  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The Burgenstock/Lake Lucerne Summit of 21&ndash;22 June 2026 constituted a formally convened face-to-face session between US Vice President JD Vance and Iranian Parliament Speaker Ghalibaf, hosted by Qatar and Pakistan as named mediators, confirmed by Reuters, Al Jazeera, NPR, and CNBC. A 60-day road-map MOU was signed specifying three working groups — nuclear issues, sanctions relief, and a monitoring mechanism — with the MOU signing date of 17 June confirmed by Al Jazeera and Axios; Iran MFA's de facto lifting of the 1 June negotiating suspension was carried in the same reports. A 24 June ceasefire declaration attributed to unnamed US and Iranian officials, covering the Strait of Hormuz, appeared in CNBC and NBC reporting; CENTCOM issued no confirmation as of end-of-day 25 June. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>
      <p>CENTCOM's press release of 16 June confirmed commencement of a new mine-countermeasure phase in the Strait of Hormuz; USS Frank E. Petersen Jr. (DDG-121) and USS Michael Murphy (DDG-112) are in active mine-clearance roles alongside underwater drone assets, per Bloomberg, Naval News, and The Hill. CENTCOM reported approximately 55 total vessel transits on 20 June; NBC reported 25 transits on 23 June; IMF PortWatch data through 21 June recorded approximately 5 commercial vessels per day against a 7-day average of 2.57 through 14 June. CENTCOM and NBC figures almost certainly include military and escort tonnage not counted under PortWatch's commercial-vessels-only definition; six P&amp;I clubs have not reinstated Hormuz war-risk coverage, with premiums remaining at eight times normal levels per Fortune (20 June). → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
      <p>On 7&ndash;8 June 2026, IDF and IRGC conducted reciprocal strikes within a 24-hour window: the Israeli Air Force launched missile attacks on Iranian military sites in Tehran, Isfahan, and Tabriz, and Iran subsequently launched ballistic missiles targeting Ramat David Airbase &mdash; confirmed damaged &mdash; within the same window. CNN, NPR, and Al Jazeera carried live coverage; both IDF and IRGC issued official statements. The six P&amp;I clubs that withdrew Hormuz war-risk coverage did so in the immediate aftermath of the Israeli strikes and have not reinstated since. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
      <p>The Knesset dissolution bill passed its first reading 106&ndash;0 on 2 June 2026, per JPost and Times of Israel. Second and third readings remained unscheduled as of 25 June; Netanyahu has reportedly prepared to push the bill through within days ahead of a preelection recess expected around 16 July, per Times of Israel (~21 June). The Haredi bloc &mdash; Shas and UTJ &mdash; sustained its plenum boycott; a date dispute between Netanyahu (late October election) and Haredi parties (September) remained unresolved. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>
      <p>President Pezeshkian stated on 23 June that Iran "will never back down from the right to enrich uranium" and separately excluded ballistic missile development from the current negotiating framework. Iran MFA Spokesperson Araghchi reiterated that IAEA inspector access to Fordow and Natanz is contingent on sanctions-relief sequencing being agreed first &mdash; a position IAEA Director General Grossi described as incompatible with standard verification practice in separately reported remarks. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

