  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode &middot; ~200 words &middot; verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media &mdash; IRNA, Tasnim, IRIB &mdash; framed the Burgenstock summit as a "defensive diplomatic necessity" imposed by US military pressure, not as a sovereign foreign-policy choice. Editorials in Kayhan on 23 June labeled the 60-day road map a "temporary ceasefire of words" and called on the Supreme Leader to refuse enrichment concessions. Tasnim ran counter-programming in parallel: Araghchi's Geneva-side readouts on 22 June, without mentioning Ghalibaf's role or the Burgenstock bilateral format. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>IRIB television broadcast multiple segments on 23&ndash;24 June featuring IRGC commanders describing Hormuz mine placement as a "sovereign act of deterrence," entirely separate from the diplomatic track. Ghalibaf's 23 June statement &mdash; that Iran would administer Hormuz "on its own terms" &mdash; was carried prominently by Fars, Tasnim, and state television without the qualifier that CENTCOM had disputed the 20 June Iranian closure declaration. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Press TV and IRNA ran English-language reports on 24&ndash;25 June foregrounding Pezeshkian's enrichment-rights statement as the definitive Iranian position heading into the working-group sessions. Coverage did not reference Pezeshkian's exclusion of ballistic missiles from the negotiating agenda or the IAEA sequencing dispute between Araghchi and IAEA Director General Grossi. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Kayhan's editorial on 25 June described the ceasefire declaration as "conditional" and specified that the IRGC retains authority to enforce Hormuz closure "at the Supreme Leader's discretion." Iranian semi-official polling data from ISPA (Iran Student Polling Agency), circulated on a closed Telegram channel, reportedly showed 61 percent of respondents opposed to nuclear enrichment concessions; ISPA figures are not independently verified by Western sources. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

